# Founding Charter attestation verifier

A static page (`index.html`) and an ES module (`verifier.js`) that prove the Founding Charter attested on chain matches its published texts: given an EAS attestation UID on Base or Base Sepolia, it reads the attestation, fetches every text from IPFS, recomputes its sha256 and shows match or mismatch per text.

```js
decodeCharterData(dataHex) -> { version, date, names, digests, cids }
verifyCharter({ uid, network, rpc, ipfs }) -> Promise<{ ok, reason?, attestation: { uid, attester, time, revoked }, texts: [{ name, cid, expected, actual, match, error? }] }>
```

- Schema: `string version,string date,string[] names,bytes32[] digests,string[] cids`; digests as `0x` + 64 lowercase hex.
- Reads EAS `getAttestation(bytes32)` (selector `0xa3112a64`) on `0x4200000000000000000000000000000000000021` with one `eth_call`; `network` is `base` (8453) or `base-sepolia` (84532) and picks the chain and the default public RPC.
- `rpc(method, params)` and `ipfs(cid) -> Uint8Array` are injected: the page passes fetch based versions (public RPC, IPFS gateway the user can change), the tests pass their own.
- `ok` is true only if the attestation exists, is not revoked and every text matches. A missing UID, a revoked attestation, an unfetchable text, an RPC failure or unreadable data are reported in `reason` or per text `error`, never thrown.
- No dependency, no build step, no key, no wallet: hand written strict ABI reader (every offset and length bounds checked) and WebCrypto sha256 (browser and Node 20).
- The page is mobile friendly (viewport meta, single column, max-width layout, long hashes wrap at 360 px), accepts `?uid=...&network=...` in the URL and escapes everything it displays.

